            ":question/problem": "Given that $f(r,\\theta)=2 r^{3}$, find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates.",
            ":question/worked-solution": "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂r}\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta}\\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r}=6 r^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(6 r^{2})\\cos \\theta-\\frac{1}{r}(0)\\sin \\theta \\\\ & =6 r^{2} \\cos {\\theta}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
